MIND \nEdited \nby Jerome \nBarkow, \nLeda Cosmides, \nand John Tooby, \nOxford: \nOxford \nUniversity \nPress, \n1992 \nThis is an important \nbook. \nSeveral \nof its authors \nhave made original \ncontributions \nto evolutionary \ntheory \n(e.g., \nDaly 1978; Orians \n1979; \nCosmides \nand Tooby \n1981; Tooby \n1982); and many \nhave studied \nbehavior \nin nonhuman \nspecies, \nincluding \nnonhuman \nprimates \n(Feistner, \nMcGrew, \nSymons), \nkangaroo \nrats (Daly, \nWilson), \nblackbirds \n(Orians), \nand dolphins \n(Mann). \nThat makes \nthem uniquely \nqualified \nto bring evolutionary \ntheory \nto the study of psychology \n(see too Crawford, \nKrebs, \nand Smith \n1987). \nThese \npapers \ncover \ntopics \nfrom social exchange \ncognition, \nto mate choice \nand retention, \nto pregnancy \nsickness, \nto language, \nto visual \nperception; \nthe perspectives \nare often new, the work is often scholarly, \nand the \nconclusions \nare often persuasive. \nBut not persuasive \nenough. \nContributors \nto this book use \n“selectional-thinking” \n(Symons, \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGrace K. Babson Collection of Newtonia | Babson Centennial (https://centennial.babson.edu/past/grace-k-babson-collection-of-newtonia/)\nciteturn22search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; A full history of the priority dispute between Newton and Leibniz concerning the invention of the calculus is not possible here, but the role of the Analysis in the affair demands a short account.\n“De analysi” thereby became the first systematic discussion of the calculus by Newton to circulate in public, and is thus one of the founding documents in the history of modern mathematics.\n\nContaining the text of two of Newton’s earliest works, as well as his first two published treatises, the Analysis serves as a wonderful starting place for those studying the history of the calculus.\n\nThe Priority Dispute\n\nThe Priority Dispute Click to Open\n\nA full history of the priority dispute between Newton and Leibniz concerning the invention of the calculus is not possible here, but the role of the Analysis in the affair demands a short account.\n\nThe story may be said to begin with the composition of the “De analysi” which, as mentioned above, was given to Isaac Barrow in 1669 and copied by John Collins.\n\nWhen Leibniz independently began to work on the calculus in 1675, he was soon not only corresponding with Newton, through third parties, but also corresponding with Collins. Collins even showed Leibniz his copy of “De analysi.”\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNewton’s De Analysi and the priority dispute∗ (https://www.lab.twcu.ac.jp/~osada/De_Analysi_Scientiarum_rev.pdf)\nciteturn22search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; In the priority dispute, Newton suspected that Leibniz had read ... In 1715, in his anonymous An Account of the Book, ... History of Science Society of Japan on 29 May 2022.\nNewton’s De Analysi and the priority dispute∗\nNaoki Osada†\nAbstract\nIn 1669 Newton wrote De Analysi to claim the priority of the\nmethod of inﬁnite series. Newton did not want to publish his method\nof ﬂuxions, so he introduced the moment instead of the ratio of ﬂux-\nions, which is the diﬀerential quotient in modern calculus, and ex-\npressed the ﬂuent, which is the antiderivative in modern calculus, in\nterms of the pair of the indeﬁnite region drawn by the motion of the\nordinate and its signed area.\nIn the priority dispute, Newton suspected that Leibniz had read\nDe Analysi in 1676, so he used De Analysi as evidence that New-\nton was the ﬁrst inventor of the method of ﬂuxions and that Leibniz\nhad plagiarized diﬀerences from the moments of De Analysi. How-\never Newton did not use ﬂuxions when he wrote De Analysi and the\nmoment in De Analysi was diﬀerential quotient, not diﬀerential, in\nmodern calculus. In 1715, in his anonymous An Account of the Book,\nNewton explained how he had represented the ﬂuxions in De Analysi\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n0195101073.pdf (https://api.pageplace.de/preview/DT0400.9780195356472_A23603640/preview-9780195356472_A23603640.pdf)\nciteturn22search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.3 years ago; THE ADAPTED MIND ... BarkowLeda CosmidesJohn Tooby\nTHE ADAPTED MIND\n\nEvolutionary Psychology and the Generation of Culture\n\nJerome H. Barkow  \nLeda Cosmides  \nJohn Tooby--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCiNii Books - マインドタイム : 「未来思考」「過去思考」「現在思考」で最高のチームをつくる (https://ci.nii.ac.jp/ncid/BB18654176)\nciteturn22search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.6 years ago; Crawled: last month;   *     * Furey, John ... It's all about time ... by \"BOOK database\"\n\n# マインドタイム : 「未来思考」「過去思考」「現在思考」で最高のチームをつくる\n\n  *     * Furey, John\n\n  *     * 花塚, 恵 ハナツカ, メグミ\n\n  *     * 別所, 美穂 ベッショ, ミホ\n\n  *     * 二宮, 美樹 ニノミヤ, ミキ\n\n  *     * 伊藤, 守 イトウ, マモル\n\n## Author(s)\n\n  *     * Furey, John\n\n  *     * 花塚, 恵 ハナツカ, メグミ\n\n  *     * 別所, 美穂 ベッショ, ミホ\n\n  *     * 二宮, 美樹 ニノミヤ, ミキ\n\n  *     * 伊藤, 守 イトウ, マモル\n\n## Bibliographic Information\n\nマインドタイム : 「未来思考」「過去思考」「現在思考」で最高のチームをつくる\n\nジョン・フューレイ著 ; 花塚恵, 別所美穂, 二宮美樹訳\n\nディスカヴァー・トゥエンティワン, 2015.4\n\nOther Title\n    \n\nmindtime\n\nIt's all about time\n\nマインドタイム : 未来思考過去思考現在思考で最高のチームをつくる\n\nTitle Transcription\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNewton's <i>De Analysi </i>and the Priority Dispute (https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/historiascientiarum/32/1/32_17/_article/-char/en)\nciteturn22search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Second Series: International Journal of the History of Science Society of Japan ... In the priority dispute, Newton suspected that Leibniz had read De Analysi in 1676, so he used De Analysi as evidence that Newton was the first inventor of the method of fluxions and that Leibniz had plagiarized differences from the moments of De Analysi. ... In 1715, in his anonymous An Account of the Book, Newton explained how he had represented the fluxions in De Analysi and rewrote the note he had given in De Analysi regarding the unit of moment so that moment meant differential.\nNewton did not want to publish his method of fluxions, so he introduced the moment instead of the ratio of fluxions, which is the differential quotient in modern calculus, and expressed the fluent, which is the antiderivative in modern calculus, in terms of the pair of the indefinite region drawn by the motion of the ordinate and its signed area. In the priority dispute, Newton suspected that Leibniz had read De Analysi in 1676, so he used De Analysi as evidence that Newton was the first inventor of the method of fluxions and that Leibniz had plagiarized differences from the moments of De Analysi. However Newton did not use fluxions when he wrote De Analysi and the moment in De Analysi was differential quotient, not differential, in modern calculus. In 1715, in his anonymous An Account of the Book, Newton explained how he had represented the fluxions in De Analysi and rewrote the note he had given in De Analysi regarding the unit of moment so that moment meant differential.\n\n\nFigures (0)\n\nImage Content from these authors\n\nSupplementary material (0)\n\nResult List ()\n\nCited by (0)\n\n© 2022 The History of Science Society of Japan--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nleibniz calculus book (https://explore.gcts.edu/calculus-suggest-005/Book?dataid=Vbj80-4911&title=leibniz-calculus-book.pdf)\nciteturn22search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year;   leibniz calculus book: The History of the Priority Di∫pute between Newton and LeibnizThomas Sonar, 2018-04-12 This book provides a thrilling history of the famous priority dispute\ntwo visionaries on the world of mathematics and the intellectual h